1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johann_Rudolf_Czernin_von_und_zu_Chudenitz
2 CONT
2 CONT Johann Rudolf Graf Czernin von und zu Chudenitz (born or baptized on the 9 June 1757 in Wien; died or buried on 23 April 1845 in Vienna) was a k. u. k. Austrian civil servant and theater director. He descended from the old Bohemia
2 CONC n noble family Czernin von und zu Chudenitz. He was the son of Count Prokop Adalbert Czernin, who offered Mozart in 1776 a one year's rent, provided he would write some compositions for the Count's orchestra. His sister was Counte
2 CONC ss Antonie Lützow. And perhaps most important his deceased mother was the sister of Archbishop Colloredo, thus the Count was a nephew of the Salzburg Archbishop. [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Czernin went to school in Salzburg, where his uncle, Count Hieronymus von Colloredo was the bishop. The young Czernin and certainly his sister were in connection with Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart (and almost the same age), who wrote a 
2 CONC Violin- and Pianoconcerto for them. Countess Lützow, 25 years old, was a fine pianist and Mozart composed a Piano Concerto (KV. 246) for her in 1776. Count Johann Rudolph Czernin was an aspiring violinist.[1] 1778 he founded an Or
2 CONC chestra, which played on Sunday afternoon at the Lodron family.[2] Leopold Mozart sent Wolfgang, then in Paris, an amusing account of its first meeting, at which both Leopold and Mozart’s sister Nannerl played.[3] Leopold wrote of
2 CONC  Czernin's limited achievements as and indefatigable violin player.[4] In 1781 he married Theresa Schoenborn and traveled with her to Italy, Switzerland, France, Belgium, Holland and England, and became interested in the new fashi
2 CONC on, the English landscape garden.[5] At the ending of the 18th century Johann Rudolf Czernin made the Hunting lodge Jemčina very famous. In the presence of representatives of the Bohemian and Austrian aristocracy famous Cursorial 
2 CONC hunting were held here, and scholars and scientists from the Bohemian National Patriotic Movement invited as guests.[6] In 1810 Goethe was invited at Krásný Dvůr Castle, the family estates, near Karlsbad.[7] In the meantime, he be
2 CONC gan to accumulate paintings and drawings, and had twenty years later, the most important collection of the Austrian Empire. In 1813 he bought The Art of Painting, a genre work by Johannes Vermeer.[8] From 1817 until 1825 František
2 CONC  Tkadlík was the court painter for the Czernin family and appointed as the guard of their art gallery in Vienna. In 1823 Czernin was appointed president of the Academy of Fine Arts in Vienna. This office he held until 1827. As ear
2 CONC ly as 1823 he was appointed by the Emperor Francis II. He was entrusted with the leadership of the imperial collections of the Court, but also the Burgtheater on Ringstrasse was under his direction. He also founded the Society of 
2 CONC the Patriotic Museums. Johann Rudolf Czernin, Count von und zu Chudenitz died at the age of 88. His private art collection at his death consisted of nearly 2,000 engravings, which laid the foundation of the Czernin Collection. Par
2 CONC ts of this collection can still be seen in the Residenzgalerie in Salzburg.
