1 NAME Myrtle /Vacirca/
2 GIVN Myrtle
2 SURN Vacirca
2 NICK Mirtella
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 29 NOV 1916
2 ADDR
3 CITY Boston
3 STAE Massachusetts
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 JAN 2012
2 ADDR
3 STAE Maryland
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Myrtle was the daughter of Vincezo Terranova Vacirca (Nov 26 1886-Dec 25 1956 Rome, Italy) and Chiara 'Clara' Palumbo* (1883 Italy- 1962 Lackawanna Co. PA?). Her birth name was Mirtella. Her father was a newspaper 
2 CONC publisher in Italy who also served with the OSS during World War II. Both of her parents were political activists. Her sister was Virginia Brown Musial.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the paternal granddaughter of Antonio Vacirca (abt 1865 Bari- ?) and Virginia Terranova (1868 Vittoria, Ragusa- 19?). Antonio was the son of Vincenzo Vacirca and Lucia Ventura. Lucia was the daughter of Baron Ventura (baro
2 CONC ne di Ventura). Her maternal grandparents were Olimpia  Monticelli della Valle and Consalvo Palumbo. They were all born in Chiaramonte, Sicily.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cesare Monticelli della Valle, the 5th Duke of Ventignani (Jan 14 1824-Feb 15 1900) and Cristina Imperali (1840-April 10 1901) may have been Myrtle's maternal great-grandparents. Cristina Imperali was the daughter of Carlo Imperal
2 CONC i (March 16 1798- Dec 12 1766, son of Vincenzo, the 5th Prince of Francavilla Sept 3 1738- Sept 3 1816), and Josephine Marullo (born 1818). Cesare's grandparents or great grandparents were Cesare Monticelli della Valle and Anna Ni
2 CONC nfa Valignano  (daughter of Frederico Valignano, the Marchese of Cephaghetti). Despite her aristocratic background, Myrtle and her family worked diligently for democracy and social justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Myrtle graduated from Brooklyn College and later the University of Palermo. Upon graduation in New York in 1936, Myrtle became a labor organizer with the  United Mine Workers. In 1939 she married an American freedom fighter and Sp
2 CONC anish Civil War veteran, Robert Jennings Quinn (1919-1943).  After the attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941, Robert joined the Royal Air Force before President Roosevelt had even declared war. Myrtle left her job with the union and went
2 CONC  to work in a converted factory in Trenton, New Jersey as a defense worker. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Several months after her husband's plane was shot down in the Mediterranean, Myrtle decided she wanted a more direct role in the war. In February 1943, she enlisted at New York's Whitehall Street Recruiting Depot. Due to her short
2 CONC  stature, she needed two waivers to pass her physical. She served as a member of the Women’s Army Corps and was initially assigned as an administrative specialist for a training flight in the Army Air Corps in San Antonio. Soon af
2 CONC ter she was reasssigned to base headquarters where she managed daily operations.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Office of Strategic Services (OSS), America’s first overseas intelligence and military special operations agency, was looking for Americans who spoke European languages and who had previously lived overseas. Myrtle was sent to
2 CONC  Daytona, Florida to undergo training. She worked two months in civilian offices in Washington, DC before she was assigned to an OSS group that would deploy to Algeria by ship to assist in planning the invasion of Italy. She share
2 CONC d an office with the staff of Charles deGaulle collecting intelligence as an OSS analyst and sometimes doing field work. Her interpreting and intelligence gathering helped Allied Forces succeed in 'Operation Tidal Wave,' which des
2 CONC troyed Nazi Germany’s oil production sources in Romania.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the capture of Mussolini and the end of World War II, Myrtle was discharged from the US Army and reassigned as a civilian OSS worker in Italy. In 1945, she single handedly pressured the US Department of State to grant the pay
2 CONC ment of death gratuities to widows and surviving families of Italian partisans killed in the line of duty during OSS missions in Italy. She stayed in Rome and helped close down the OSS operations in Italy in 1947. Myrtle used her 
2 CONC G.I. benefits to earn a juris doctor degree from the University of Palermo in 1951. During this time she met Francesco Alliata Di Villafranca (1919-2013 Sicily), an Italian newspaperman and filmmaker. Alliata did not want to move 
2 CONC to the US, so Myrtle took their two children and returned home in 1956. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She then spent two years employed at the Italian consulate before starting a career that spanned three decades with the New York City Department of Social Services Division of Employment and Rehabilitation. She retired in 1987 but
2 CONC  continued to stay connected with her political causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 2015, largely due to the efforts of freelance writer and veteran's advocate Luis Carlos Montalván, Myrtle was awarded a Congressional Certificate which states:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In recognition of your invaluable contributions in the U.S. Army, Women’s Army Corps during World War II while serving with the Office of Strategic Services (OSS), the predecessor agency of the Central Intelligence Agency in the 
2 CONC Italian Theatre of Operations, and your exemplary trailblazing performance set the stage for increasing roles of women in the United States military in the later part of the 20th  century and beyond.”                              
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT Note-Towards the end of her life Myrtle took pleasure in stylizing her name as Vacirca-Quinn. Her legal name was probably Vacirca at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT For a more descriptive and complete story about Myrtle, please see online articles by Luis Carlos Montalván (some of the information about Myrtle's service was extracted from  his articles):
2 CONT
2 CONT ~Remembering One Woman’s War
2 CONT ~Finally Honoring 'One Woman’s War'
